Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus. They asked him, "Who is the most important person in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus came and called a little child, and commanded him to stand among them.
Jesus said, "Truly, I tell you, unless you change and become like little children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Everyone who becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
Whoever welcomes a little one such as you in my name, welcomes me.
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me were to cause one of these little ones to sin? If he does that, it would be better for him if a large millstone were hung around his neck and he were thrown into the depths of the sea.
Oh, the horror that will befall the world because of the things that lead people into sin! Such things must come. But oh, the horror for those who cause it!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot, than to go to hell with both hands and feet. In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The parable of the lost sheep.
Look, don't look down on any of these little ones. I tell you, their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father in heaven.
<.>
What do you think? Suppose a man has 100 sheep, and one of them goes astray. Would he not leave the 99 sheep on the hills? Would he not go and search for the one that went astray?
What I am telling you is the truth. If that sheep is found, he will be happier with this sheep than with 99 sheep that did not escape.
And the same is true for your Father in heaven; He does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When someone wrongs you.
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Keep the matter between the two of you. If he listens to you, you have him back.
But what if he will not listen to you? Then take with you one or two more, that every matter may be established by the testimony of two or three witnesses (Deuteronomy 19:15).
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then say it to the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Do not treat him as your brother. Treat him as you would treat an unbeliever or a tax collector.
What I tell you is truth. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Again, here's what I say. Suppose two of you on earth agree on anything you ask for. My Father in heaven will do it for you.
"Where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them." The servant who had no mercy"
Peter came to Jesus. He asked, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to collect all the money that was owed to him by his servants.
When the king started doing that, a man who owed him millions of dollars was brought in.
The man was unable to pay. Therefore, his master issued a command. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed were to be sold to pay his debt.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Grant me time," he pleaded. "I will restore everything."
His master felt pity for him. He forgave him his debt and allowed him to depart.
But then that slave went out and found one of the other servants who owed him a few dollars. He grabbed him and began to strangle him. "Pay what you owe me!" he said.
The other servant fell on his knees. "Grant me time," he pleaded. "I will pay you."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. The man would be held there until he could repay his debt.
The other servants saw what happened. This disturbed them greatly. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the master called the first servant. "You wicked servant," he said. "I forgave you all that you owed me because you pleaded with me.
Isn't it your duty to have mercy on the other servant, just as I have had mercy on you?
In a rage, his master handed him over to the guards. He would be punished until he repaid all his debts.
This is how my father who is in heaven will treat each and every one of you, unless you forgive your brother from your heart."
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
A large crowd followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They said, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator made them male and female? (Genesis 1:27)"
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and they shall become one flesh" (Genesis 2:24)
They are no longer two, but one. Therefore, what God has joined together, let no one separate."
They asked: "Then why did Moses command that a man write a certificate of divorce for his wife and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses permitted you to divorce your wives because you were stubborn. But it was not like that from the beginning.
This is what I am telling you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery. A man is not permitted to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him."
The disciples said to him, "If such is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who have been helped to live without marriage can accept it.
Some men are unable to have children because they were born that way. Some may have been born that way by the hands of others. And others may have made themselves that way in order to serve the kingdom of heaven. And whoever is able to accept living this way, should do so." The little children are brought to Jesus."
Some people brought their little children to Jesus. They wanted him to place his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them. For the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he went from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus. He asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to inherit eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "For there is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, then hear the commandments."
"And he said, 'What of it?' asked the man. Jesus said, 'You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness.'"
And honor your father and your mother. (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) And you shall love your neighbor as yourself. " (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "I have obeyed all those commandments. What do I still need to do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Jesus said to his disciples, "Truly I tell you, it is difficult for those who are wealthy to enter the kingdom of heaven."
And I tell you again, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle. But it is easier for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God."
When the disciples heard this, they were truly astonished. They asked, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible for man is possible with God."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What will be given to us?"
Jesus said to them, "What I tell you is true. When everything is reformed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. Then, those who have followed me will sit on 12 thrones. And you will judge the 12 tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left their home or their family or their field because of me will receive 100 times more. And they will also receive eternal life.
But many of the first shall be last; and many of the last shall be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who is a landowner. He went out early in the morning to hire people to work in his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine o'clock in the morning he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also go to my vineyard, and I will give you what is right."
They left. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock and did the same thing.
Around five o'clock, he went out and found others standing there. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day and doing nothing?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. "He said to them, 'Go also you, and work in my vineyard.'"
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the one who was in charge of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with the others who were hired last. Then go on to the first ones."
The workers who were hired arrived around five o'clock. Each of them received the usual daily wage.
When those who had been paid first came, they left so that they could take more. But each one of them also received the wage for his ordinary day.
When they received it, they began to suspect the owner.
They said, "These people you've recently hired have only worked for one hour. You paid them the same amount you paid us. We did most of the work and were under the hot sun all day."
The owner answered one of them, "My friend, I am being fair with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give the same salary I gave you to those I recently hired.
Do I have the right to do whatever I want with my money? Do you feel cheated because I gave generously to others?
"Therefore, the last will be first, and the first will be last." Jesus speaks once again about his coming death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
He said, "We will go up to Jerusalem." "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will give him to people who are not Jews. They will make a mockery of him and flog him. They will crucify him on a cross. On the third day, he will rise from the dead!" A mother asks Jesus to give her help.
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus. Her sons came with her. She knelt down and asked Him to grant her request.
Jesus asked her, "What do you want?" She said, "Promise me that one of my sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom. Promise that the other will sit at your left hand."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" And they said, "We can."
Jesus said to them, "You will surely drink from my cup. But it is not for me to say who will sit at my right hand or at my left. These places belong to those for whom my Father has prepared them."
The other ten disciples heard about this; they were angry at the two brothers.
Jesus called them together. He said, "You know about the rulers of the Gentiles; for they exercise authority over their people, and they make their officers sit down for the purpose of judging the people who are beneath them. But it shall not be so among you; but whoever desires to become great among you, let him be your servant; and whoever desires to be first among you, let him be your slave, even as the Son of Man was not to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many."
Do not be like that. Instead, every one of you who wants to be important must make yourselves servants.
And whoever wants to be first must be the slave of all.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many people." Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed him.
There were two blind men sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, saying, "Lord, son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They told them to be quiet. But the two men cried out even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them, saying, "What do you want Me to do for you?" He asked.
"Oh Lord," they answered, "we want to see."
Jesus felt deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. Immediately they could see. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
When they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. As soon as you enter it, you will find a tethered female donkey, with its foal by its side. Untie them and bring them both to me."
If someone tells you something, say the Lord needs them. The owner will send them immediately."
And this was to fulfill what had been spoken by the prophet. As it says:
Tell the city of Zion: "Behold, here is your king! He comes to you, righteous and victorious, lowly and riding on a donkey, even on a colt, the foal of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them to do.
Bring the donkey and the young donkey. They put their clothes on them. Then Jesus sat on the clothes.
Large crowds of people in their garments stretched out on the road. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some of the people went ahead of him, and some followed him. They all cried out, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the entire city was in an uproar. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowds answered, "This is Jesus. He is the Prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple area. He began to drive out those who were buying and selling there. He also overturned the tables of those who were exchanging money. He also overturned the benches of those who were selling doves.
He said to them, "It is written, 'The Lord has said, "My house shall be called a house of prayer"'" (Isaiah 56:7). "But you are making it into 'a den of robbers.'" (Jeremiah 7:11)
The blind and lame people came to Jesus at the Temple. There he healed them.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They saw him heal the sick, make the lame walk, and even raise the dead. They also saw the children in the temple area shouting "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw all this, they were indignant.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked Him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read in the Scriptures, 'The very young women and even the children praise You?'" (Matthew 21:16, quoting Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the people and went out of the city to Bethany. And he abode there that night. And in the morning, as he returned into the city, he hungered. And when he saw a fig tree in the way, he came to it and found nothing thereon but leaves only. And he said unto it, Let no fruit grow on thee henceforward for ever. And immediately the fig tree withered away.
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the roadside. He went to it but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "Perhaps you will not bear fruit again!" Suddenly the tree dried up.
When the disciples saw this, they were amazed. "How did the fig tree dry up so quickly?" they asked.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must believe and not doubt. Then you can do what they did to the tree. And you can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' It will be done."
If you believe, you will receive what you ask for when you pray." Jesus' authority is called into question.
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus said, "I will ask you one question. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did the baptism of John come? From heaven or from men?" And they began to reason and say among themselves, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say "of the men"? We fear people. Everyone thinks that John is a prophet.
They answered Jesus, "We do not know." Jesus said to them, "Then I cannot tell you by what authority I do these things. The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? A man with two sons went to the first son and said, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
"I will not do that," the son answered. But he changed his mind later and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. And the son replied, "I will go, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did what his father wanted?" They answered, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly I tell you, the tax collectors and the prostitutes will enter the kingdom of God ahead of you.
John came to show you the right way to live. And you didn't believe him. But tax collectors and prostitutes did. I saw this. But even then you didn't leave your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned a piece of land, so he planted a vineyard. He put a wall around it. He dug a well for the wine press in it. And he built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to some farmers. Then he went on a journey.
When the harvest was near, he sent his servants to the tenants. He told the servants to gather his portion of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw stones at the third to kill him.
Then the man sent other slaves to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at the first. But the tenants treated them no differently.
Finally, he sent his son to them. "They will respect my son," he said.
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will get all the owner's property someday. Let's kill him. Then everything will be ours."
So they took him, brought him out of the vineyard, and then killed him.
When the vineyard owner returns, what will he do with those tenants?
"'These evil ones he will destroy,' they answered. 'Then he will rent the vineyard to other tenants. They will give him his share of the crop at harvest time.'"
Jesus said to them: "Have you not read what the Scriptures say: 'The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone. This was the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)"
Here is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you. And it will be given to people who will produce its fruit.
Anyone who falls on those stones will be crushed to pieces. But the stone will crush anyone who falls on it."
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard about Jesus's stories. They knew he was talking about them.
They searched for a way to arrest him, but they feared the crowd. For the people were believing that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
Thus is the kingdom of heaven. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his slaves to those who had been invited to the dinner. The slaves told them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent some other servants. "Tell those who have been invited that I have prepared my dinner. I have killed my ox and my finest livestock. Everything is ready. Come to the wedding dinner."
But the people paid no attention. One went to his field. Another went to his work.
The rest seized his servants. They treated them harshly and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed the murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding feast is ready. But those I invited were not suitable to attend."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you can find.
The slaves went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they could find, whether they were good or bad. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not dressed in wedding clothes.
He said, "My friend, how did you get in here without wedding clothes?" The man was unable to think of anything to say.
Then the king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet. Throw him outside into the darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and plotted how they might entangle Jesus in His own words.
They sent their followers to him. They sent the Herodians with them. "Teacher," they said, "we know that you are a good and honest man. You truly understand God's way. You do not let others tell you what to do or say, no matter how important they are."
Tell us then, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar, or not?"
But Jesus knew their evil plans. He said, "You slanderers! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin that people use to pay the tax." So they brought him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Who is in this picture? And who are these words?"
"For Caesar," they answered. Then He said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they went away from him and left him. Marriage when the dead rise.
And on that day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people are raised from the dead.
They said, "Teacher, thus did Moses speak to us. If a man should die, having no children, then his brother shall take the wife of the deceased, and raise up up children for his brother, that his name may not be blotted out from Israel."
We had seven brothers. The first one got married and died. Since he didn't have children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same accident happened to the second and third brothers. It continued until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Now, when the dead rise, to whom will a wife belong? All seven were married to her."
Jesus said, "You are wrong, because you do not know the scriptures. And you do not know the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry. Nor will their parents allow them to marry. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead coming to life? Have you not read what God has told you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. And I am the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead. He is the God of the living."
When the crowd heard this, they were amazed at his teachings. The most important commandment
When the Pharisees heard that the Sadducees had not been able to respond to Jesus, they assembled.
One of them was an expert in the law. So he tested Jesus with a question.
"Teacher," he asked, "which is the most important commandment in the Law?"
Jesus said, "You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind." (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: You shall love your neighbor as yourself. (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the Law and the Prophets is based on these two commandments." Son of whom is Christ?
The Pharisees met. Jesus asked them:
What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he?" "The son of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Why is David called 'Lord'? The Holy Spirit spoke to David himself. David said,
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet." (Psalm 110:1)
"If David calls him 'Lord,' how can he be David's son?"
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus came to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The scribes and Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
You must obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But do not do what they do. For they do not practice what they preach.
They burden people with heavy burdens and place them on the shoulders of others. However, they themselves do not wish to move them, even with a single finger.
All that they do is done for others to see. On their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing texts from the Bible. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the strings on their coats very long.
They like to sit in the place of honor at dinners. They also like to get the most important seats in synagogues.
They like to be greeted in the markets. They like it when people call them "my Lord."
But do not call anyone on earth 'Rabbi,' for you have only one teacher, and all of you are brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth 'father.' You have one Father, and He is in heaven.
You should not be called "Teacher." You have one Teacher, the Christ.
Your server will be the most important person among you.
Whoever exalts himself will be humbled. And whoever is humbled will be exalted.
Oh, you teachers and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You lock people out of the kingdom of heaven. You don't enter, and you prevent others from entering.
<.>
Oh, if only you teachers and Pharisees! You scoundrels! You travel over land and sea to make a single convert to your religion. And then you make him twice as much a child of hell as you yourselves are.
"Oh ye blind guides! Ye say, 'If a man swear by the temple, it is nothing; but if he swear by the gold of the temple, he must needs fulfill his oath.' "
You are blind and ignorant! Which is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the Temple, which makes the gold sacred?
As you say, 'If anyone swears by the altar, it is nothing; but if anyone swears by the gift that is on it, he is bound to keep his oath.'"
O ye blind! Which is more important? The gift? Or the altar which makes the gift sacred?
Whoever swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And whoever swears an oath by the name of the temple, swears an oath by its name and by whatever dwells in it.
And everyone who swears an oath in the name of heaven swears an oath in the name of God's throne and of the one who sits upon it.
"Oh, you hypocrites! You teachers of the law! You are snakes, you brood of vipers! How can you escape being condemned to hell? Therefore, I tell you that the kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a people who will produce its fruits. For the people of the kingdom will be thrown out into the darkness, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. "Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. You who are listening to me, take this to heart: If those who do not believe in me continue to listen to my words, they will be given eternal life. For my words are spirit and life."
I blind you! You take out the smallest insect from your food. But you swallow a whole camel!
Oh, if only you, you teachers of the law and you Pharisees! You are the ones who are hypocrites! You clean the outside of the cup and the dish. But on the inside you are full of greed. You want only to satisfy your own desires.
You blind guides, you hypocrites! First clean the inside of the cup and the dish; then the outside will be clean as well.
Oh, if only you teachers and Pharisees! You are the frauds! You are like tombs that are painted with whitewash. They look beautiful on the outside. But on the inside they are full of the bones of the dead. They are also full of other things that are not pure and clean.
The same applies to you. From the outside, it looks like you are doing what is right. But on the inside, you are full of what is wrong. You pretend that you are not what you are.
Oh, if only you, teachers of the law and Pharisees! You snakes! You build tombs for the prophets. You decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And We said, 'If only we had been in the days of those who were before us, we would not have been associators and we would not have been among the Muslims, and we would not have been aiding the hypocrites.'
You testify against yourselves. You acknowledge that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
Desist from the sins with which those who lived before you began!
Your nest is made of poisonous snakes! How will you survive the judgment by going to hell?
I will send you prophets and wise men and teachers. And some of them you will kill. And some of them you will crucify. And some of them you will torture in your temples. And you will pursue them from city to city.
Therefore you will pay the price of the blood of all righteous people who have been shed on earth. I mean from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah the son of Berechiah. Zechariah was the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I am telling you is the truth. All of this will happen to those who are living now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. How often I have wanted to gather your people, just as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
End Times Signs
Jesus went out of the temple. And as he was going away, his disciples came near to him. They wanted to call his attention to the buildings of the temple.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "What I tell you is true. Not one stone here will be left on another. Every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, the disciples came to him in secret. "Tell us," they said. "When will this happen? And what will be the sign of your coming? What will be the sign of the end?"
Jesus answered, "Beware! Beware of letting anyone deceive you."
Many will come in my name. They will say, "I am the Christ!" They will deceive many people.
You will hear of wars. And you will hear also of wars to come. Do not be troubled. Those things must happen. But the end is not yet.
Nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. There will be great earthquakes, famines and pestilences in various places.
All of these are the beginning of labor pains.
Then people will be handed over to you so that you may mistreat them and kill them. And all nations will hate you because of me.
In that time, many will depart from their faith. They will hate one another. Some will hand over others to their enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because evil will grow, the love of most people will diminish.
But the one who stands firm to the end will be saved.
These joyful news will be announced to the Kingdom throughout the whole world. It will be a testimony to all nations. Then, the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that destroys" (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) In the future day you will see it standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Then those in Judea must flee to the mountains.
No one on the surface should go down to their home to take anything from it.
Nobody in the field should have to go back for their coat.
How terrible it would be in those days for pregnant women! How terrible it would be for nursing mothers!
Pray lest you be forced to flee in winter or on the Sabbath.
There will be terrible suffering in those days. It will be worse than anything else that has happened in the world from the beginning of time until now. And something like this will never happen again.
If the watch is not shortened, no one will survive. But because of God's chosen people, her time will be shortened.
At that time, someone might say to you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or "Here he is!" Do not believe it.
Christians and false prophets will appear. They will perform great miracles. They will attempt to deceive God's chosen ones if possible.
Look, I already told you.
If someone tells you, "He is far in the desert," do not go there. Or if someone tells you, "He is deep in the house," do not believe it.
Lightning, seen approaching from the east, will be visible in the west. Likewise, when the Son of Man comes, it will be the same.
Vultures will gather wherever there is a carcass.
Immediately after the terrible suffering of those days, "the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light; the stars will fall from the sky, and the heavens will be shaken." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
At that time, the sign of the Son of Man will appear in the sky, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven. He will come with power and great glory.
He will send His angels with a loud trumpet call. He will gather His chosen people from the four corners of the earth. They will bring them from one end of the heavens to the other.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. When its branches become tender and it puts forth leaves, you know that summer is near.
Likewise, when you see all those things happening, you know the end is near. It's right at the door.
What I am telling you is true. The people who are alive at that time will not die until all those things have happened.
The heaven and the earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away. No man knows the day or the hour.
No one knows about that day or hour. Even the angels in heaven do not know. The Son does not know. Only the Father knows.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
Before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day that Noah entered into the ark.
They did not know anything about what would happen until the flood came and took them all. That is how it will be when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in a field. One will be taken and the other left.
Two women will grind food using a hand mill. One will be taken and the other will be left.
Therefore be ready, for you do not know on what day your Lord is coming.
You must understand something. Suppose the homeowner knew at what time of night the thief would come. Then he would remain vigilant. He would not allow his house to be broken into.
Be you also ready; for the Son of Man will come in a hour that you do not expect.
Suppose a master puts one of his slaves in charge of the other servants in his house. The slave's task is to give them their food at the proper time. The master wants a faithful and wise slave for this purpose.
It will be good for the slave if his master finds him doing his work when the master returns.
What I am telling you is the truth. The master will put that servant in charge of everything he owns.
But suppose that this slave is wicked; suppose that he says to himself: "My master will be absent for a long time."
Suppose he begins to strike the other servants. And suppose he eats and drinks with those who drink heavily.
The master of that slave will return on a day the slave does not expect. He will return in an hour the slave does not know.
Then the master cuts him into pieces. And sends him to the place where the protesters go. There people will weep and grind their teeth.
