Judas, who had handed Jesus over to die, found that Jesus had been condemned to death. He felt profound shame and sorrow for what he had done. Therefore, he returned the 30 pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders.
He said: "I made a mistake. I handed over an innocent man." They replied: "What does it matter to us? This is your problem."
Judah then threw the money into the temple and left. After this, he went and hanged himself.
The leading priests selected the coins. "We cannot put this money into the temple coffer," they said. "It is blood money. It has paid for a man's death."
They decided to use the money to buy the pottery field. People from other countries will be buried there.
Therefore, the Field of Blood is called by this name to this day.
Then the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which the people of Israel had valued him."
They used the coins to buy the potter's field, as the Lord had instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12,13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) They brought Jesus to Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" "Yes. That is quite true," Jesus answered.
But when the leading priests and the elders brought charges against him, he made no response.
Then Pilate asked him, "Don't you hear the charges they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus answered none of the charges, not even to the single charge. The governor was truly astounded.
It was the custom of the ruler at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people could choose whom they wanted.
At that time they had a notorious prisoner named Barabbas.
When the crowd was gathered, Pilate asked them, "Whom do you want me to release for you: Barabbas, or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were envious. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judge's seat, his wife sent him a message. "Have nothing to do with this man," she said, "he is innocent. I have suffered much in a dream today because of him."
But the chief priests and the elders incited the crowd to ask for Barabbas to be released and Jesus to be killed.
The governor said, "Whom do you want me to release?" And they said, "Barabbas."
"What shall I do then with Jesus who is called Christ?" Pilate asked. And they all answered together, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What has he done wrong?" Pilate asked. But they shouted even louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was accomplishing nothing. Instead, the crowd began to grow angry. So he took water and washed his hands before them. "I am innocent of this man's blood," he said. "You are responsible for it!"
All the people answered, "We and our children will accept the blame for his death!"
With Pilate, Barabbas was released. But Jesus was flogged. Then he was handed over to be crucified on the cross. The soldiers mock Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. The rest of the soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him of his clothes and put a purple robe on him.
Then they wove the thorns together to make a crown. They put it on his head. They put a stick in his right hand. Then they fell down on their knees in front of him and mocked him. "Hail, King of the Jews!" they said.
They cursed him. They hit him on the head with a stick again and again.
After mocking him, they stripped him of his robe. They put his clothes on him. Then they led him away to be crucified on the cross. Jesus is crucified on the cross.
As they were going out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene. His name was Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
And they came to a place called Golgotha. And the word Golgotha means the place of the head.
