From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered all the land.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice. He said, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" which means "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who stood there heard Jesus cry out. They said, "He is calling Elijah."
Suddenly, someone took a cloth soaked in vinegar and put it on a stick, then offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let's see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the veil of the temple was torn from top to bottom. The earth shook. The rocks split.
The graves opened. The bodies of many holy people who had died were brought back to life.
They came out of the tombs. After Jesus rose to life, they went to the holy city. There they appeared to many people.
The Roman centurion and the soldiers guarding Jesus saw the earthquake and all that had happened. They were terrified. They exclaimed, "Truly, he was the Son of God!"
It was not far, many women were watching. For they had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was among them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was also there. So was the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus was buried.
When evening came, a rich man came from the city of Arimathea. His name was Joseph. He had become a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean piece of linen.
He placed her in his new grave which he had dug in the rock. Then he rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards of the tomb
The next day, the day following the preparation day, the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
They said, "Lord, we remember something the false witness said while he was alive. He claimed, 'I will rise again after three days.'"
So he told them to put a guard on the tomb until the third day. Otherwise, his disciples might come and take away the body. Then they would tell the people that Jesus had risen from the dead. These last lies would be worse than the first ones."
"Take with you some guard," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb secure as much as you can."
So they went and put a lock on the tomb, and placed a seal on the stone, and put the guards on duty.
Jesus rises from the dead.
Saturday is now past. The dawn was at the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to the tomb to look at it.
A strong earthquake occurred. The Angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. He rolled away the stone and sat on it.
His body is shiny like lightning. His clothes are white like snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He was not here! He has risen, just as he said he would! Come and see the place where he lay.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him." And now, I have told you."
The women hurried away from the tomb. They were afraid, but they were filled with joy. She hurried to the disciples to tell them.
Suddenly Jesus met them. "Peace be with you!" he said. So they came to him, and touched his feet and worshipped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards went and informed the chief priests.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards went into the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the chief priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
They told the soldiers: "We want you to say, 'His followers came by night. They stole his body while we slept.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we'll pay him off. This will keep you out of trouble."
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has been spread among Jews to this day. The Final Instructions of Jesus to His Disciples
Then the twelve disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain that Jesus had told them to go to.
When they saw him, they worshipped him. But some still doubted.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me.
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey all that I have commanded you. And you will be certain that I am always with you, to the end."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
The prophet Isaiah wrote long ago: "I will send my messenger ahead of you, who will prepare your way." (Malachi 3: 1)
The angel says in the wilderness: "Prepare the way of the Lord. Make his paths straight." (Isaiah 40:3)
John came. He was baptizing people in the desert. He was also preaching that people must be baptized and repent of their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the country of Judea went out to him, and all the people of Jerusalem. And they were baptized by him in the river Jordan, confessing their sins.
John was clothed in garments made of camel's hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
Thus John preached: "One mightier than I is coming after me. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of His sandals."
I baptize you with water. But he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. And suddenly, he saw the heavens being torn open. And he saw the Spirit descending on him like a dove.
He was told by a voice from heaven, "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Immediately the Spirit sent Jesus out into the wilderness.
He was in the wilderness for forty days. There his Tempter tempted him. Jesus did no harm to the wild beasts. Angels ministered unto him. Jesus chose the first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went into Galilee. He was preaching the good news of God.
He said: "The time has come, the Kingdom of God is at hand, repent of your sins and believe in the good news!"
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting their net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately, they left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little further; and when he walked, he saw James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. And they were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately he called them. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men. Then they followed Jesus. Jesus Expels an Unclean Spirit
Jesus and his disciples went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, he went to the synagogue. There he began to teach.
People were amazed at his teachings. He taught them as one who had authority. He did not speak as the teachers of the law.
Suddenly, a man in their temple screamed. He was under the control of an evil spirit. He said:
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!"
Jesus said firmly, "Be quiet!" "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a loud scream.
Everyone was astonished. They asked one another, "What is this? A new teaching! And with such authority! He even gives orders to evil spirits, and they obey him."
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus went out, having those who had been with him at the synagogue. And they went straight away with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
His mother-in-law Simon was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand and helped her up. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the town gathered at the door.
Jesus healed many of them. They were suffering from all kinds of diseases. He also drove out many demons. But He did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who He was. Jesus prayed in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they shouted: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to some other place. I want to go to the nearby towns. I must preach there also. For this I have come."
He traveled around all of Galilee. He was teaching in their synagogues. He was also casting out demons. Jesus taught a man who had a skin disease.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. On his knees, he asked Jesus. He said, "If you are willing to make me 'clean', you can do it."
Jesus was filled with profound concern. He reached out and touched the man. "I am willing to do this," he said. "Be clean!"
Immediately, he left the illness. He was cured.
Jesus immediately sent him away. And he gave the man a strong warning.
He said, "Don't tell anyone about this. Go and make yourselves visible to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. He will be a witness to the priest and the people that you are 'clean.'"
But the man went out and began to talk at once. He spread the news to everyone. So Jesus could no longer enter the city openly. He stayed outside in lonely places. But people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
And after a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had returned to his house.
Many people gathered together, so that there was no room left. Not even outside the door was there any room. And Jesus taught them the Word.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't bring him to Jesus because of the crowd. So they made a hole in the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man on his mat through the hole.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
There were some rabbis sitting there. They were thinking.
Why does this man speak in this way? He is saying something extremely evil! Only God can forgive sins!
Jesus immediately understood what they were thinking in their hearts, so he said to them, "Why are you thinking these things in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
"I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
He said, "I'll tell you, get up. Take your rug and go back to your house."
The man stood up and took his mat. Then he walked away while everyone was watching. Everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Lazarus
Jesus returned again to the side of the Sea of Galilee. A large crowd came to him. He began to teach them.
And while he was walking, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax collector's booth. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." And he got up and followed him.
After that, Jesus was having dinner at the home of Lazarus. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd that was following Jesus.
There were some teachers of the law who were Pharisees. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard that. He said to them, "Those who are in good health do not need a physician; but those who are ill do. I did not come for those who believe they are righteous before God in order to follow me. I came for sinners in order to follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
The disciples of John and of the Pharisees would fast. The disciples of John came to Jesus. They said to Him, "Why do the disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also, but Your disciples do not fast?" Jesus said to them, "The wedding guests cannot fast while the bridegroom is with them, can they? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast. But the days will come when the bridegroom is taken away from them; then they will fast in those days."
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them."
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; and in that day they will go out without eating.
People don't put new patches on old clothes. If they did, the new patch would tear away from the old. This would make the hole even worse.
People do not put new wine into old wineskins. Otherwise, the wine will burst the skins, and both the wine and the skins will be ruined. No, people put new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath."
One Sabbath day, Jesus was walking with His disciples through the grainfields. The disciples began to pluck some of the heads of grain.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look, if someone does this on the Sabbath, it is against the law. Why do your disciples do this?"
He answered, "Have you read what David did? He and his companions were hungry. They needed food."
When Abiathar was the high priest, David went into the house of God and ate of the holy bread. For it was unlawful for any but the priests to eat it. David also gave some of it to his men."
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath.
The master of the human is the master of the Sabbath day as well."
The treatment is on Saturday.
And Jesus went at another time to the synagogue. And there was a man there with a withered, weak, and crooked hand.
There were some Pharisees trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus said to the man with the withered and deformed hand, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say we must do on the Sabbath? Must we do good? Or must we do evil? Must we save life? Or must we kill?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at everyone with anger. He was very upset because their hearts were hard. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was completely new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus. They wanted to kill Jesus. The people gathered behind Jesus.
Jesus went with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee. A great crowd from Galilee followed him.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. Many people came to him. They came from Judea, Jerusalem, and Idumea. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area of Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from crowding him.
Jesus had healed many people. Therefore, the sick would crawl forward to touch him.
When the people saw those possessed by evil spirits, they fell to his feet. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain. He called some people to come to him, and they came.
Twelve of them he appointed and named them apostles. And they have been with him since. And he also sends them to preach.
They have authority over demons.
Jesus appointed the twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were also Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them too. He was the one who would later deliver Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Belial.
Jesus entered a house. The crowd gathered again. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family learned about it, so they went to take care of him. She said, "He is crazy."
There were some teachers of the law there. They came from Jerusalem. "He is controlled by Beelzebul!" they exclaimed. "By the prince of demons he drives out demons!"
Jesus called them and said to them in parables: "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights against itself, it cannot stand.
If the family splits, it cannot stand.
